## Conclusion

The revenue volatility problem at Litware Terminal Services is real, well-documented, and financially significant. Monthly revenue swings from $1.36M to $9.52M produce EBITDA margins ranging from −37.8% to +65.5%, creating cash flow instability, workforce planning challenges, and distorted equipment acquisition decisions [1]. The 2025 budget-to-actual miss — where July was budgeted at $6.12M but came in at $1.96M — demonstrates that current forecasting capabilities are inadequate for a company approaching $85M in revenue [3].

## Data Enrichment Potential

| Category | Enrichment Opportunity |
|---|---|
| **Project-level margin data** for repeat clients (bid vs. actual) | Would enable precise quantification of bid accuracy improvement opportunity |
| **Client-level revenue concentration** | Would identify which of the 29 clients are most amenable to scheduling incentives |
| **Equipment utilization rates by month** | Would quantify idle capacity during trough months and validate demand-shaping potential |
| **Detailed subcontractor cost structure** (fixed vs. variable) | Subcontractor & hauler costs are 38.5% of COGS; understanding their flexibility would refine labor-savings estimates |
| **Client contract terms** (notice periods, scheduling flexibility clauses) | Would validate whether off-peak pricing incentives are contractually feasible |
| **Competitor pricing data** during off-peak periods | Would inform whether pricing incentives can be offered without competitive disadvantage |

